\begin_layout Subsection
Importance of Preprocessing (8 points)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When measuring income in thousands of USD the nearest neighbor algorithm 
\emph on
would not
\emph default
 lent C money as person B is closest (note that any strictly increasing monotone transformation preserves ordering):
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\|(A-C)\|^{2}=(47-21)^{2}+(35-36)^{2}=677,
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\|(B-C)\|^{2}=(22-21)^{2}+(40-36)^{2}=17.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If we instead measure income in dollars the characteristics of person A is now closer to C and BoL 
\emph on
would 
\emph default
lent the money to person C:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\|(A-C)\|^{2}=(47-21)^{2}+(35000-36000)^{2}=1,000,676,
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\|(B-C)\|^{2}=(22-21)^{2}+(40000-36000)^{2}=16,000,001.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The conclusion is that choices doing preprocessing generally effects an algorithms predictions.
\end_layout

